From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: allwinner: A733 mmc0 SD UHS SDR104

License: GPL-2.0-only OR MIT (DT patch).

Turn on SD UHS (SDR50 / SDR104, 1.8 V signalling) on the A733 SD slot
(mmc0 / SMHC0, bank PF), driven by the PF I/O-voltage power-source pinconf
added in patch `pinctrl-sunxi-a733-pf-io-voltage-power-source` -- no variable vqmmc regulator.

Pin groups
----------
Split the PF pin group into two states that carry the signalling voltage
as a power-source pinconf (microvolts, the driver's unit contract):
 - mmc0_pins (default): PF0..PF5, pinmux 2, function mmc0, 3.3 V.
 - mmc0_pins_1v8: identical pins/mux/function, 1.8 V.

mmc0 controller node
--------------------
 - pinctrl-names = "default", "sdmmc-3v3", "sdmmc-1v8". The two voltage names
   are the documented Tegra pair (nvidia,tegra20-sdhci.yaml: "pad configuration
   for 3.3 V / 1.8 V"), which is exactly the semantics here. `mmc-sunxi-pad-io-voltage-switch-without-vqmmc` is what makes
   sunxi-mmc.c look them up; before it, this file had no pinctrl reference at
   all and an earlier revision of this patch invented a "sdmmc-1v8" name that
   nothing in the tree read. That cost three board sessions.
 - REMOVE vqmmc-supply. It was a fixed 3.3 V rail; mmc_regulator_set_vqmmc()
   would fail the 1.8 V request and sunxi_mmc_volt_switch() returns -EIO.
   With vqmmc absent the driver skips the regulator path and the 1.8 V
   switch is done purely by the sdmmc-1v8 pinctrl state -> power-source ->
   GPIO_POW_VAL_SET_CTL (0x0070). vmmc-supply (card power) is kept as-is.
 - add sd-uhs-sdr50 + sd-uhs-sdr104; keep cap-sd-highspeed.
 - max-frequency 150 MHz -> 200 MHz (SDR104).

⚠ **This is the half that broke the card on 2026-07-29.** With the pads still
at 3.3 V -- because the power-source property reached no driver, see `pinctrl-sunxi-parse-the-generic-power-source-property` --
advertising sd-uhs-sdr50/sdr104 made the mmc core switch the card to 1.8 V
signalling against 3.3 V pads, and the SD stopped enumerating entirely. Three
boots of one kernel isolated it: both patches = no card, `pinctrl-sunxi-a733-pf-io-voltage-power-source` + pre-patch DT =
card fine, `arm64-dts-allwinner-a733-sd-uhs-sdr104` minus the two sd-uhs lines = card fine. So the driver half, the
vqmmc removal, the sdmmc-1v8 state and 200 MHz are all harmless; these two
capability lines are the trigger, and they are only safe once the pads actually
follow.

**Test this in two steps, not one.** The kernel stage can be pointed at another
series file (A7S_KERNEL_SERIES), so the control kernel is a build with
`arm64-dts-allwinner-a733-sd-uhs-sdr104` left out: it answers "does the register move
at all" without risking the card. Only when GPIO_POW_VAL_SET_CTL is observed
changing with the sdmmc-1v8 state does the full series get a boot. If the card
disappears again, the pads are still not following and the caps come back out.

Evidence for the 1.8 V mechanism: GPIO_POW_VAL_SET_CTL (0x0070) + A733
UM v0.92 (VCC_PF_PWR_VOL_SEL, 0=1.8V/1=3.3V) + silicon readback on two 6 GiB boards,
2026-07-29 (0x0070 = 0x0 at SDR104). Pins/IRQ/CD/rails are unchanged from
patch `arm64-dts-allwinner-a733-add-sd-emmc` (already silicon-derived on the 6 GiB board and the 8 GiB board).

HW-effect PENDING: a green build / clean dtc is not proof of the hardware
effect. Unproven until a board test on the 8 GiB board shows the SD card negotiating
SDR104 at 1.8 V through these states, with 0x0070 reading 0x0 during 1.8 V
signalling.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
index 75b4da2dd..a9b29f371 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -561,6 +561,17 @@ mmc0_pins: mmc0-pins {
 				       "PF3", "PF4", "PF5";
 				allwinner,pinmux = <2>;
 				function = "mmc0";
+				/* 3.3 V default signalling. Microvolts. */
+				power-source = <3300000>;
+			};
+
+			mmc0_pins_1v8: mmc0-pins-1v8 {
+				pins = "PF0", "PF1", "PF2",
+				       "PF3", "PF4", "PF5";
+				allwinner,pinmux = <2>;
+				function = "mmc0";
+				/* 1.8 V UHS signalling. Microvolts. */
+				power-source = <1800000>;
 			};
 
 			/*
@@ -777,15 +788,32 @@ mmc0: mmc@4020000 {
 			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_SMHC0>;
 			reset-names = "ahb";
 			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 161 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
-			pinctrl-names = "default";
+			/*
+			 * The mmc core selects the "sdmmc-1v8" state (`mmc-sunxi-pad-io-voltage-switch-without-vqmmc` looks it up by
+			 * name, sunxi-mmc.c pinctrl_lookup_state) during UHS
+			 * negotiation; its power-source pinconf drives the SoC PF
+			 * voltage bit (GPIO_POW_VAL_SET_CTL) to 1.8 V, and back to
+			 * 3.3 V with the default state. No variable vqmmc rail.
+			 */
+			pinctrl-names = "default", "sdmmc-3v3", "sdmmc-1v8";
 			pinctrl-0 = <&mmc0_pins>;
+			pinctrl-1 = <&mmc0_pins>;
+			pinctrl-2 = <&mmc0_pins_1v8>;
 			vmmc-supply = <&reg_vcc3v3>;
-			vqmmc-supply = <&reg_vcc3v3>;
+			/*
+			 * No vqmmc-supply on purpose. A fixed 3.3 V vqmmc makes
+			 * mmc_regulator_set_vqmmc() fail the 1.8 V request and
+			 * sunxi_mmc_volt_switch() returns -EIO; with it absent the
+			 * driver skips the regulator path and the 1.8 V switch is
+			 * done purely by the sdmmc-1v8 pinctrl state above.
+			 */
 			cd-gpios = <&pio 5 6 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;	/* PF6 = pin 166; card-in reads HIGH on this board */
 			bus-width = <4>;
-			max-frequency = <150000000>;
+			max-frequency = <200000000>;
 			cap-sd-highspeed;
 			cap-mmc-highspeed;
+			sd-uhs-sdr50;
+			sd-uhs-sdr104;
 			#address-cells = <1>;
 			#size-cells = <0>;
 			status = "okay";
